{:squares [{:pattern "ukrns/publication-cadence" :turn "turn-w3xIu6" :cited-by "象 (2026-10-01 rationale: 'The pattern names exactly this axis')" :assignment {:IF "I1" :HOWEVER "I2" :BECAUSE "G" :THEN "B"} :status "occurrence" :schema :wm/three-halves-square-v1 :objects {:I1 {:text "The pattern operates on the axis: living document (continuously-updated canonical version) ↔ frozen snapshots (versioned fixed publications with citable identifiers). Irreducible: a single publication cannot be simultaneously maximally current AND maximally citable as a fixed object." :source {:path "futon3/library/ukrns/publication-cadence.flexiarg" :sha256 "24a8a429148b1c6e2b41e482ee539c00285154957022f18b3dab181bbdf4fe12"} :theory {:elems #{:plop-2026-html-s-living-rendering-and-its-frozen} :names {:plop-2026-html-s-living-rendering-and-its-frozen "plop-2026.html's living rendering and its frozen conference snapshot"} :axioms #{}} :situation-source "turn-w3xIu6: Joe asks the viewer to 'bring up the conference version there'"} :I2 {:text "Real costs: living costs archival durability — a snapshot citable today may differ from the same URL tomorrow; frozen costs currency — citable versions drift further from the current evidence state over time. Active dynamic: early drafts favour living (claims are forming); as claims stabilise, snapshots become appropriate; archival norms for academic work favour snapshots. + FAILURE-MODES: - Ambiguous citation: \"the paper\" cited by URL changes silently; citing reader sees different content than intended - Living-without-history: current version has no revision history; prior states irrecoverable - Snapshots-without-revisions: snapshots exist but do not declare their evidence-revision; cannot be reproduced - Stale-snapshot-only: the most recent snapshot is years old; the self-application claim is unverifiable - Citation-ambiguity-unowned: the project has not decided what readers should cite; different citations proliferate" :source {:path "futon3/library/ukrns/publication-cadence.flexiarg" :sha256 "24a8a429148b1c6e2b41e482ee539c00285154957022f18b3dab181bbdf4fe12"} :theory {:elems #{:the-viewer-serves-only-the-living-rendering-so-t} :names {:the-viewer-serves-only-the-living-rendering-so-t "the viewer serves only the living rendering, so the conference version cannot be cited from the address"} :axioms #{}} :situation-source "turn-w3xIu6"} :B {:text "Operate an explicit move on the named axis (see substructure below); steward the pattern against the declared discipline. + COMPOSITIONS: (1) Living-only: current HTML (or notebook) with visible revision history; no frozen snapshots. Gives up archival citability. (2) Snapshot-only: versioned PDFs or archived HTML with DOIs at each publication event; intermediate states not published. Gives up currency. (3) Both: living current version + periodic frozen snapshots, each snapshot declaring its evidence-file revision and compute-date. Gives up simplicity; serves both readerships. Working papers typically need (3); the paper-draft-16-april is an instance of an intermediate snapshot (marked by date in filename). + CHECK: Engaged when: readers can find both the current state and the most recent citable snapshot; each snapshot declares its evidence-file revision and compute-date; the composition is declared. Failing when: \"the paper\" is ambiguous — readers citing different URLs think they are citing the same document; snapshots exist but do not declare their evidence-revision; the living document drifts without visible history." :source {:path "futon3/library/ukrns/publication-cadence.flexiarg" :sha256 "24a8a429148b1c6e2b41e482ee539c00285154957022f18b3dab181bbdf4fe12"} :theory {:elems #{:the-conference-version-served-at-plop-2026-html-} :names {:the-conference-version-served-at-plop-2026-html- "the conference version served at plop-2026.html with the living version still findable"} :axioms #{}} :situation-source "turn-w3xIu6; CHECK: engaged when readers can find both"} :G {:text "Citation integrity: an academic artefact that readers cite must be citable as a specific object. Snapshots give citation integrity; living documents do not. A paper that is both a living document AND cited in grant applications requires snapshots for the citation paths. Currency integrity: a paper that argues for design-as-function-of-evidence and is five years out of date undercuts its own argument. The living document is what demonstrates the self-application claim. Reader-need differentiation: different readers want different things. A funder reviewing a proposal wants the current state; a peer reviewer citing the work in a rebuttal wants a fixed target. Composition (3) serves both. Cross-pattern: publication-cadence is downstream of ukrns/design-state-lineage (snapshots are checkpoints in the lineage) and a sibling of ukrns/cycle-position-publication (both publish design states at chosen intervals). Mechanic, in short: Mechanic: how the artefact is published (living, snapshot, both). Serves: self-application's artefact-instantiation claim. Axis: living document ↔ frozen snapshots. Without this pattern: \"which paper\" is ambiguous; self-application cannot be demonstrated." :source {:path "futon3/library/ukrns/publication-cadence.flexiarg" :sha256 "24a8a429148b1c6e2b41e482ee539c00285154957022f18b3dab181bbdf4fe12"} :theory {:elems #{:the-one-address-plop-2026-html-read-by-both-citi} :names {:the-one-address-plop-2026-html-read-by-both-citi "the one address plop-2026.html read by both citing and current readers"} :axioms #{}} :situation-source "CASCADE-SPEC-v3 §2 (c17 reading, 2026-10-05)"}} :maps {:a1 #{[:the-one-address-plop-2026-html-read-by-both-citi :plop-2026-html-s-living-rendering-and-its-frozen]} :a2 #{[:the-one-address-plop-2026-html-read-by-both-citi :the-viewer-serves-only-the-living-rendering-so-t]} :b1 #{[:plop-2026-html-s-living-rendering-and-its-frozen :the-conference-version-served-at-plop-2026-html-]} :b2 #{[:the-viewer-serves-only-the-living-rendering-so-t :the-conference-version-served-at-plop-2026-html-]}} :auxiliary {:b1 false :b2 false} :check "Engaged when: readers can find both the current state and the most recent citable snapshot; each snapshot declares its evidence-file revision and compute-date; the composition is declared.\n      Failing when: \"the paper\" is ambiguous — readers citing different URLs think they are citing the same document; snapshots exist but do not declare their evidence-revision; the living document drifts without visible history."}
 {:pattern "pattern-interpretation/cite-the-source-bytes" :turn "turn-zZ52wx" :cited-by "retrieval (score 0.5428)" :assignment {:IF "I1" :HOWEVER "I2" :BECAUSE "G" :THEN "B"} :status "occurrence" :schema :wm/three-halves-square-v1 :objects {:I1 {:text "A pattern must become executable guards and effects." :source {:path "futon3/library/pattern-interpretation/cite-the-source-bytes.flexiarg" :sha256 "6e0ccc53582f2413cf03deae983af553d91fa1cd86e952dda18c1ae8f5d01337"} :theory {:elems #{:the-wm-blend-square-producer-want-interpretation} :names {:the-wm-blend-square-producer-want-interpretation "the WM blend-square producer, want_interpretation/validate-response"} :axioms #{}} :situation-source "futon2 src/futon2/aif/want_interpretation.clj:260"} :I2 {:text "A compiler over words is cheap and covers little; an agent's reading is broad but unverifiable unless tied to the text." :source {:path "futon3/library/pattern-interpretation/cite-the-source-bytes.flexiarg" :sha256 "6e0ccc53582f2413cf03deae983af553d91fa1cd86e952dda18c1ae8f5d01337"} :theory {:elems #{:the-legacy-migration-template-and-seat-authored-} :names {:the-legacy-migration-template-and-seat-authored- "the legacy migration template and seat-authored square texts on the 2026-10-14 trace"} :axioms #{}} :situation-source "futon2 data/wm-trace/wm-trace-2026-10-14.edn; legacy_three_halves_registry.clj"} :B {:text "Bind each clause of the interpretation to quoted, sha-pinned source bytes and make that record the single reading." :source {:path "futon3/library/pattern-interpretation/cite-the-source-bytes.flexiarg" :sha256 "6e0ccc53582f2413cf03deae983af553d91fa1cd86e952dda18c1ae8f5d01337"} :theory {:elems #{:the-parts-check-refusal-at-the-producer} :names {:the-parts-check-refusal-at-the-producer "the parts-check refusal at the producer"} :axioms #{}} :situation-source "packet to codex-2, job invoke-1792193935835-15912-5c902ffb"} :G {:text "The quote is the one part of a translation that can be checked by identity; everything else is checked against it." :source {:path "futon3/library/pattern-interpretation/cite-the-source-bytes.flexiarg" :sha256 "6e0ccc53582f2413cf03deae983af553d91fa1cd86e952dda18c1ae8f5d01337"} :theory {:elems #{:the-pattern-file-bytes-at-their-sha256} :names {:the-pattern-file-bytes-at-their-sha256 "the pattern file bytes at their sha256"} :axioms #{}} :situation-source "futon3/library/<family>/<name>.flexiarg"}} :maps {:a1 #{[:the-pattern-file-bytes-at-their-sha256 :the-wm-blend-square-producer-want-interpretation]} :a2 #{[:the-pattern-file-bytes-at-their-sha256 :the-legacy-migration-template-and-seat-authored-]} :b1 #{[:the-wm-blend-square-producer-want-interpretation :the-parts-check-refusal-at-the-producer]} :b2 #{[:the-legacy-migration-template-and-seat-authored- :the-parts-check-refusal-at-the-producer]}} :auxiliary {:b1 false :b2 false} :check "Every interpretation quotes and cites the pattern's clauses to its sha-pinned bytes, and both prediction and enactment read that one record."}
 {:pattern "cascade-construction/lift-when-three-align" :turn "turn-zZ52wx" :cited-by "retrieval" :assignment {:IF "I1" :HOWEVER "I2" :BECAUSE "G" :THEN "B"} :status "occurrence" :schema :wm/three-halves-square-v1 :objects {:I1 {:text "Their rows appear to line up." :source {:path "futon3/library/cascade-construction/lift-when-three-align.flexiarg" :sha256 "06a12356f46905c436669464aa36e99e114361e3da907176a009a55a772757a9"} :theory {:elems #{:wm-blend-squares-citations-and-m40-facts-carryin} :names {:wm-blend-squares-citations-and-m40-facts-carryin "WM blend squares, 象 citations and m40 facts carrying the same four parts"} :axioms #{}} :situation-source "turn-zZ52wx: 'we will be working with similar data structures'"} :I2 {:text "Abstraction is tempting after two, and the third instance may be only a proposal." :source {:path "futon3/library/cascade-construction/lift-when-three-align.flexiarg" :sha256 "06a12356f46905c436669464aa36e99e114361e3da907176a009a55a772757a9"} :theory {:elems #{:wm-and-have-written-squares-m40-holds-none} :names {:wm-and-have-written-squares-m40-holds-none "WM and 象 have written squares; m40 holds none"} :axioms #{}} :situation-source "m40_find publication-cadence, 2026-10-16: one memory, no square"} :B {:text "Wait for three written instances, then lift with gaps kept." :source {:path "futon3/library/cascade-construction/lift-when-three-align.flexiarg" :sha256 "06a12356f46905c436669464aa36e99e114361e3da907176a009a55a772757a9"} :theory {:elems #{:the-side-occurrence-records-as-the-third-written} :names {:the-side-occurrence-records-as-the-third-written "the 象-side occurrence records as the third written instance"} :axioms #{}} :situation-source "futon3c/scripts/occurrence_square.py output"} :G {:text "The shared pattern is useful to a reader only if its @why can be seen working in each concrete @how." :source {:path "futon3/library/cascade-construction/lift-when-three-align.flexiarg" :sha256 "06a12356f46905c436669464aa36e99e114361e3da907176a009a55a772757a9"} :theory {:elems #{:cascade-spec-v3-2-five-rules} :names {:cascade-spec-v3-2-five-rules "CASCADE-SPEC-v3 §2 five rules"} :axioms #{}} :situation-source "futon2 e5c3a4604"}} :maps {:a1 #{[:cascade-spec-v3-2-five-rules :wm-blend-squares-citations-and-m40-facts-carryin]} :a2 #{[:cascade-spec-v3-2-five-rules :wm-and-have-written-squares-m40-holds-none]} :b1 #{[:wm-blend-squares-citations-and-m40-facts-carryin :the-side-occurrence-records-as-the-third-written]} :b2 #{[:wm-and-have-written-squares-m40-holds-none :the-side-occurrence-records-as-the-third-written]}} :auxiliary {:b1 false :b2 false} :check "Lift to a shared pattern from three written, aligned cascades, with a checklist @how, one incident per instance and recorded gaps."}
 {:pattern "peripherals/pattern-becomes-peripheral" :turn "turn-zZ52wx" :cited-by "retrieval" :assignment {:IF "I1" :HOWEVER "I2" :BECAUSE "G" :THEN "B"} :status "occurrence" :schema :wm/three-halves-square-v1 :objects {:I1 {:text "The patterns are correct and the problem they address is real: there exists a productive way of working that is not being practiced." :source {:path "futon3/library/peripherals/pattern-becomes-peripheral.flexiarg" :sha256 "8b69ee2b641365114775d4b4e07b11049c87adfdece638173e39dea3ce26f472"} :theory {:elems #{:cascade-spec-v3-2-five-rules} :names {:cascade-spec-v3-2-five-rules "CASCADE-SPEC-v3 §2 five rules"} :axioms #{}} :situation-source "futon2 e5c3a4604"} :I2 {:text "Correctness does not produce inhabitation. A pattern describes the right envelope but does not address whether anyone enters it or whether it evolves through use. The pattern sits in the library (or the programme design document, or the mission file) as a description of what would work — and the world continues not to work that way. This is not a failure of the pattern. It is a category gap. Pattern theory gives you the vocabulary for describing *what works under what conditions*. What it does not give you is the space in which the working happens, the surface that earns entry into that space, or the loop through which the space improves. Those are peripheral-theoretic concerns. The gap shows up identically across scales: - A developer has 800 patterns in a library but reaches for the CLI because the REPL (which instantiates the patterns as a working environment) has too much friction. - An AI agent has a proof peripheral defined with phases and exit conditions but one-shots every problem because entering the peripheral is harder than not entering it. - A national programme has five well-grounded design patterns but the institutional architecture that would instantiate them as lived practice is largely absent. In all three cases, the patterns are correct. In none of them is the pattern doing work. The pattern is a description; it has not yet become a space. + FAILURE-MODES: - Pattern-as-commandment: \"you must use the proof peripheral\" produces compliance, not inhabitation. The agent enters the space under obligation and exits at the first opportunity. - Pattern-as-prohibition: \"you must not use the CLI\" blocks the alternative without making the peripheral attractive. Produces resentment and workarounds. - Absorbed input mistaken for success: the trainer delivered, the code was written, the problem was solved. But nothing was learned at the system level. Output without capability is the most common and least visible failure. - Blame allocated to inhabitants: \"the trainers didn't embed the programme\" / \"the agent didn't use the peripheral\" / \"the developer didn't use the REPL.\" The diagnostic should be: the space didn't earn inhabitation, the surface had too much friction, or the Baldwin loop was starved." :source {:path "futon3/library/peripherals/pattern-becomes-peripheral.flexiarg" :sha256 "8b69ee2b641365114775d4b4e07b11049c87adfdece638173e39dea3ce26f472"} :theory {:elems #{:defaulted-squares-on-the-2026-10-14-trace-writte} :names {:defaulted-squares-on-the-2026-10-14-trace-writte "defaulted squares on the 2026-10-14 trace, written and never read against the pattern"} :axioms #{}} :situation-source "futon2 data/wm-trace/wm-trace-2026-10-14.edn"} :B {:text "Recognise that pattern theory is necessary but not self-completing. The bridge from pattern to practice is *instantiation as peripheral*: the pattern must become a constrained execution envelope (what the space contains), with a surface that earns inhabitation (why anyone enters), feeding a Baldwin loop (how the space evolves from use). This bridge has a distinctive character that distinguishes it from both commandment and prohibition. The pattern-as-documentation says \"here is what works\" — a descriptive claim. A commandment (\"thou shalt use the proof peripheral\") tries to force entry through obligation. A prohibition (\"thou shalt not use the CLI\") tries to force entry by blocking alternatives. Neither works, for reasons the philosophical tradition has long understood: - Spinoza: joy (laetitia) is an increase in the power to act. A peripheral that earns inhabitation produces joy — the inhabitant finds their power to act increased by being inside it. A commandment produces obedience, which is not joy. A prohibition produces resentment or evasion, which is the opposite. - Nietzsche: affirmation is the only durable motivation. The peripheral must be affirmed — chosen because it is good, not endured because it is required. - Sen: capability is freedom, not functioning. The peripheral must create real freedom to act — conversion factors that turn nominal access into durable capability. A programme that provides access without conversion factors provides a pattern without a peripheral. The transition from pattern to peripheral is therefore not an implementation step (\"now deploy the pattern\"). It is a creative act: the construction of a space that is *worth inhabiting*, that increases the inhabitant's power to act, and that generates the data needed to evolve itself. Crucially, this means the moral weight falls on the *space*, not on the *inhabitant*. When a trainer doesn't embed the programme, or an agent doesn't use the peripheral, or a developer doesn't use the REPL, the diagnosis is not \"the inhabitant is deficient\" but \"the space has not yet earned inhabitation.\" This is not moral grayness — it is a precise structural claim: the conditions for productive action have not been met, and the responsibility lies with whoever designs and maintains the conditions, not with whoever fails to thrive within inadequate ones." :source {:path "futon3/library/peripherals/pattern-becomes-peripheral.flexiarg" :sha256 "8b69ee2b641365114775d4b4e07b11049c87adfdece638173e39dea3ce26f472"} :theory {:elems #{:the-parts-check-refusal-at-the-producer} :names {:the-parts-check-refusal-at-the-producer "the parts-check refusal at the producer"} :axioms #{}} :situation-source "packet to codex-2"} :G {:text "The absorbed-input mode reveals why this matters. In the absorbed case, an individual gains something — skills, experience, CV lines, working code — but the system gains nothing. The trainer leaves and takes the knowledge with them. The agent writes code but no institutional learning accumulates. The developer uses Stack Overflow and gets a working function but no understanding of why it works. What doesn't happen in the absorbed case is the creation of new institutions (Ostrom). The trainer doesn't create a community of practice at their institution. The agent doesn't produce evidence that improves the proof peripheral. The developer doesn't contribute a pattern back to the library. The individual transacts with the system but the system does not change. This is the \"programming by Stack Exchange\" failure mode applied universally. An interaction that produces output without producing capability is an interaction that has been *absorbed* rather than *institutionalised*. The output is real — the code works, the training was delivered, the problem was solved — but it is non-generative. Nothing was learned at the system level. No new institution was created. No peripheral evolved. The pattern-to-peripheral bridge is what converts absorbed input into institutional creation. When a pattern becomes a peripheral: 1. The envelope defines a positive action space (not a prohibition). 2. The surface earns entry (not commands it). 3. Inhabitation generates data (not just output). 4. The data feeds back into the peripheral's design (Baldwin loop). 5. The improved peripheral earns further inhabitation (virtuous cycle). At step 5, something new exists that didn't exist before: an institution in Ostrom's sense — a set of rules-in-use that govern a shared resource (the action space) and that evolve through the participation of their inhabitants. This is what the absorbed-input mode fails to produce, and it is what the pattern-to-peripheral transition is for. The Spinozan frame is the deepest one: the question is not \"did the agent obey the pattern?\" but \"did the agent's power to act increase?\" If it did, the pattern has become a peripheral. If it didn't — if the agent merely transacted and moved on — the pattern remains documentation, however correct it may be. + evidence: M-repl-wins-over-cli (futon3c, 2026-03-29): the pattern library contained patterns for evidence logging, frame persistence, and coordination. None were operative until the REPL surface was made inhabitable. The patterns became peripherals only when the basics (interrupt, tool visibility, cursor stability) were fixed. + evidence: M-apm-solutions (futon3c, 2026-03-29): the proof peripheral defined phases, criteria, and exit conditions. Claude-1 one-shot every problem. The pattern was correct; the peripheral was not inhabited. + evidence: UKRN ORP T3 programme (paper_v2.4): five well-grounded design patterns, backed by NPT coding and transcript evidence. The patterns accurately described what would work. The institutional architecture that would instantiate them as lived practice was largely absent. Absorbed-input mode: trainers gained individual skills but no new institutions were created." :source {:path "futon3/library/peripherals/pattern-becomes-peripheral.flexiarg" :sha256 "8b69ee2b641365114775d4b4e07b11049c87adfdece638173e39dea3ce26f472"} :theory {:elems #{:seat-authored-square-text-that-no-later-check-re} :names {:seat-authored-square-text-that-no-later-check-re "seat-authored square text that no later check reads"} :axioms #{}} :situation-source "futon2 data/wm-interpretations/M-futonzero-mvp.edn"}} :maps {:a1 #{[:seat-authored-square-text-that-no-later-check-re :cascade-spec-v3-2-five-rules]} :a2 #{[:seat-authored-square-text-that-no-later-check-re :defaulted-squares-on-the-2026-10-14-trace-writte]} :b1 #{[:cascade-spec-v3-2-five-rules :the-parts-check-refusal-at-the-producer]} :b2 #{[:defaulted-squares-on-the-2026-10-14-trace-writte :the-parts-check-refusal-at-the-producer]}} :auxiliary {:b1 false :b2 false} :check "A pattern that remains documentation has not yet become a peripheral. The transition from pattern to peripheral is the transition from describing what works to being the space in which the working happens — and that transition is what creates new capability, not merely new knowledge."}]
